      ":question/math-academy-id": "q-183786",
      ":question/problem": "Use mathematical induction to prove that $6(n+1)<{n}^{3}$ for $n\\ge 3,n\\in N.$\n\nWe proceed using induction on $n.$\n\n**Base Case**: We first prove that our statement is true for the base case $n=\\,${{field-1}}.\n\nFor the left-hand side, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{{field-2}}={{field-3}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is\n{{field-4}}\nthe right-hand side.\nThus, our statement is {{field-5}} for $n=3.$\n\n**Inductive Hypothesis**: We assume that for some $n=$ {{field-6}}, we have\n\n$$\n{{field-7}}<{{field-8}}.\n$$\n\n**Inductive Step**: To prove $P(k+1),$ we write\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6(k+2) & ={{field-9}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy the inductive hypothesis, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6(k+1)+6 & <{{field-10}} \\\\  & ={{field-11}} \\\\  & <{{field-12}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & ={{field-13}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nas required.\n\nTherefore, by the principle of induction, $6(n+1)<{n}^{3}$ is true for all\n{{field-14}}."
    },
