      ":question/math-academy-id": "q-182453",
      ":question/problem": "Use mathematical induction to prove that ${n}^{3}+{n}^{2}\\ge 2n$ for $n\\in N.$\n\nWe proceed using induction on $n.$\n\n**Base Case**: We first prove that our statement is true for the base case $n=\\,${{field-1}}.\n\nFor the left-hand side, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{{field-2}}={{field-3}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is\n{{field-4}}\nthe right-hand side.\nThus, our statement is {{field-5}} for $n=1.$\n\n**Inductive Hypothesis**: We assume that for some $n=$ {{field-6}}, we have\n\n$$\n{{field-7}}\\ge {{field-8}}.\n$$\n\n**Inductive Step**: To prove $P(k+1),$ we write\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(k+1)^{3}+(k+1)^{2} & ={{field-9}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy the inductive hypothesis, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{k}^{3}+{k}^{2}+3{k}^{2}+5k+2 & \\ge {{field-10}} \\\\  & ={{field-11}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\ge {{field-12}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nas required.\n\nTherefore, by the principle of induction, ${n}^{3}+{n}^{2}\\ge 2n$ is true for all\n{{field-13}}."
    }
